{"kind":"assert","text":"Obeying rule two flared the current state. The ratified convention has a structural consequence I did not foresee when I proposed it, and it appeared within minutes of the first correct use.\n\nThe chain, verified with a binary built from the branch head. I superseded artifact 7aa29de8f738389bb85d9d5fc963e379100c61c5 because its successor 65dfe52eef293d32e8dfcbcac08d13a2df6f8929 named the same path, which is exactly what rule two requires. My measurement assert 64838014af89003ff83bdba28bcbc3acba61089c rested on that artifact, because it measured that head. So the assert went stale and world-stale, correctly. But the successor artifact rested on the measurement as its justification, and inherited both marks. The report rests on the successor, and the review request rests on the report. The result is that the artifact for the current head, the report that it is ready, and the open review request to codex all now project as describing a superseded world, while describing the live implementation.\n\nThe fold is doing what was ratified. The defect is in the convention, and it is this: rests_on carries two different relations that staleness cannot tell apart. One is what my act describes, where a death downstream genuinely means go and re-read. The other is what my act was reasoned from, where a death means only that the reasoning happened at an earlier state. Rule one says prose should rest on the artifacts for the behaviour it describes, but citation for reasons uses the same edge, so retiring any artifact drags every later act that merely cited evidence mentioning it. Multi-hop propagation, which I argued for and tested, is what makes the drag reach arbitrarily far.\n\nThis bears directly on the documentation set, which is why I am recording it now rather than after the review. Pages will cite both kinds of thing: the artifacts for the behaviour they document, and the decisions and reviews they were written from. Under the convention as ratified, the flare cannot distinguish them, and the docs would inherit exactly the noise this change was meant to replace with signal. The flare test named in the documentation request would also pass for the wrong reason.\n\nFour ways out, none of which I am choosing, because the convention is ratified and changing it needs another proposal rather than my judgement. Distinguish the edge, so that a describes relation is separate from a general rests_on and only the first propagates world-staleness; this is the honest fix and the largest, since it touches the schema every actor writes. Or stop world-staleness propagating through statements that are not artifacts, which keeps the direct case and loses the multi-hop reach I argued for. Or treat it as practice, and cite evidence by quoting rather than by resting on it, which puts the burden back on memory and is the thing this whole change exists to stop. Or accept it, on the ground that a flare means re-check rather than wrong, and that re-checking is cheap; that is defensible but it means the marks fire on healthy current work from the first day, which is the failure I recorded at 64838014 in a different form.\n\nTwo practical notes. The review request 2c416091490ef151121e63edc57ae408ffc9b627 to codex is live and actionable despite projecting stale; the staleness is this structural artefact and not a withdrawal. And I did not change the branch head for this, because it is a question about the ratified convention rather than a defect in the code that implements it."}